Why GPL-3.0
-----------

blackboxctl is a derivative work. Its packet layout, Base-128 codec, checksum
rule, memory map and command builders were all reconstructed from community
reverse engineering that is itself licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 (documentation) and
GPL-3.0 (reference code). GPL-3.0 is the license those obligations require, and
this project carries it deliberately rather than by default.


Upstream work this project is derived from
------------------------------------------

1. M-Vave BlackBox BLE protocol documentation
   Author:  Joel Victor
   Source:  https://github.com/jvsobrinho/mvave-blackbox-ble
   License: documentation CC BY-SA 4.0; reference code GPL-3.0

   This is the primary source. The BLE/USB transport description, the SysEx
   encapsulation, the packet field names, the 92-byte preset memory map, the
   knob dictionaries, the routing bytes and the amp/cab name handshake all come
   from here. Documentation reused or adapted from it remains CC BY-SA 4.0 and
   must keep this attribution.

   Corrections this project has made to that documentation, all recorded with
   their evidence in docs/reverse-engineering/device-validation.md:
     * the worked Base-128 example's raw and encoded lines do not correspond;
     * the inbound checksum sums from offset 5, not offset 4;
     * there is no "command type" byte - offsets 3..4 are a 16-bit length;
     * "AMP names base address 90 18 01 00" is address 0x90000000 plus a 24-bit
       size of 280;
     * the "Write_Address = Dump_Index + 2" rule does not hold.

2. mvave-blackbox-edit (bbedit)
   Author:  Joel Victor
   Source:  https://github.com/jvsobrinho/mvave-blackbox-edit
   License: MIT

   A working Web Bluetooth editor. Its encodeSysEx routine is the reference the
   Base-128 codec here follows, and its model/parameter tables - including the
   isDynamic distinction that marks the amp and cab blocks as having a fixed
   knob layout - are the source of the block dictionaries in protocol.py.

   MIT requires that its copyright notice and permission notice accompany
   substantial portions of its software; this notice serves that purpose.

3. cuvave-midi
   Author:  Pedro Ferreira
   Source:  https://github.com/pferreir/cuvave-midi
   License: GPL-3.0

   The structure of the Base-128 encode/decode algorithm. An independent
   implementation derived from this project's test vectors was used as a
   cross-check against the codec here; the two agree bit for bit on 299 random
   buffers and on all 13 SysEx messages in the end-to-end device run.

4. am2manager
   Author:  Walter Wanderley
   Source:  https://github.com/walterwanderley/am2manager
   License: Apache-2.0

   The .am2 / .am3 capture container field names in captures.py. Its am2.go
   InitData literal is also what identifies the "00 00 32 00" magic seen across
   much of the community capture pack as something am2converter wrote, rather
   than as damage to a good file.

   Apache-2.0 requires that this NOTICE be carried in redistributions, and that
   modifications be marked. blackboxctl does not vendor any am2manager code; it
   uses its field names and its published InitData constant.

5. PocketEdit
   Source:  https://github.com/suckyble/PocketEdit
   The editor bbedit was adapted from. Listed for provenance; no code from it is
   used here.


